WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.224 --> 00:00:15.086
On a warm night in August of 1921, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, also known as FDR, suddenly felt tired and decided to go to bed early.

2
00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:25.763
Vacationing on Campobello Island in Canada, the Roosevelt family had enjoyed a day of sailing and swimming, but by the next day, FDR had developed a high fever.

3
00:00:25.764 --> 00:00:33.495
Within weeks, FDR discovered he had polio, a highly infectious disease for which there was no vaccine or cure.

4
00:00:33.496 --> 00:00:39.208
From that moment, FDR knew that he would never walk, or even stand, without assistance.

5
00:00:39.209 --> 00:00:52.085
Fate had dealt a crushing blow to the 39-year-old politician, yet he would go on to inspire millions of people and become the only man to serve four consecutive terms as president of the United States.

6
00:00:52.086 --> 00:00:59.292
In 1882, FDR was born in Hyde Park, New York, into one of the wealthiest families in America.

7
00:00:59.293 --> 00:01:02.903
He was an independent boy who loved riding and sailing.

8
00:01:02.904 --> 00:01:10.615
Every winter, the Roosevelts would stay in their New York City townhouse, and in summer, they would vacation on Campobello Island.

9
00:01:10.616 --> 00:01:16.488
FDR was privately tutored and then attended boarding school at the age of 14.

10
00:01:16.489 --> 00:01:21.669
He entered Harvard University four years later and graduated in just three years.

11
00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:29.050
FDR greatly admired his fifth cousin, Theodore Roosevelt, who had been the 26th president of the United States.

12
00:01:29.051 --> 00:01:43.307
FDR entered state politics in 1910, but within two years, President Woodrow Wilson appointed him Assistant Secretary of the Navy, a position Theodore Roosevelt himself had held from 1897 to 1898.

13
00:01:43.308 --> 00:01:52.349
FDR proved himself an effective leader, and in 1916, he expanded American naval power by creating the United States Navy Reserves.

14
00:01:52.350 --> 00:02:03.025
Bright and talented, FDR was nominated to be the Democratic candidate for vice-president in 1920, and his popularity soared as Americans heard him on the campaign trail.

15
00:02:03.026 --> 00:02:12.955
But the Republican Party won the White House, and in 1921, after the grueling campaign across the country, FDR took a long-overdue vacation.

16
00:02:12.956 --> 00:02:18.175
At Campobello, doctors delivered the news that FDR would never again walk unaided.

17
00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:23.395
Polio was common around the world and killed or paralyzed thousands of people every year.

18
00:02:23.396 --> 00:02:29.228
FDR, determined not to surrender to paralysis, explored every type of therapy.

19
00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:39.499
In 1924, a friend told him about a resort in Warm Springs, Georgia, which had naturally heated mineral springs that his friend thought might help ease FDR's symptoms.

20
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:50.145
FDR visited Warms Springs and was so impressed with the recuperative effects of the water that he turned it into a rehabilitation center for polio victims in 1927.

21
00:02:50.146 --> 00:02:56.415
He later founded the March of Dimes, an organization that raised money to eradicate the disease.

22
00:02:56.416 --> 00:03:04.178
In 1928, FDR wanted to run for governor of New York, but he worried about the public's perception of his physical disability.

23
00:03:04.179 --> 00:03:11.178
Fearing voters would consider him too weak to hold public office, FDR decided to conceal the true extent of his condition.

24
00:03:11.179 --> 00:03:15.805
He made campaign speeches from the back of a car with a hidden iron bar.

25
00:03:15.806 --> 00:03:21.494
When FDR wanted to address a crowd, he would grab the bar and pull himself up to a standing position.

26
00:03:21.495 --> 00:03:25.724
Then he would snap his metal leg braces into place to hold himself up.

27
00:03:25.725 --> 00:03:32.600
Newspaper photographers, by a gentleman's agreement, did not photograph FDR in a way that would reveal his disability.

28
00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:41.416
Unbelievably, of the tens of thousands of photographs taken of FDR throughout his life, only two exist that show him in his wheelchair.

29
00:03:41.417 --> 00:03:50.257
Soon after being elected governor, FDR faced a crisis when the American stock market crashed in 1929, and the Great Depression began.

30
00:03:50.258 --> 00:03:55.549
Bank failures, unemployment, hunger, and homelessness affected millions of Americans.

31
00:03:55.550 --> 00:04:10.247
So FDR decided to run for president of the United States; he believed the government should directly help suffering Americans by providing economic relief—something the administration of the current president, Herbert Hoover, refused to do.

32
00:04:10.248 --> 00:04:16.135
In 1932, FDR began the first of his four terms as president of the United States.

33
00:04:16.136 --> 00:04:25.064
He calmed and gave hope to the nation in his inaugural address when he spoke the memorable words "the only thing we have to fear is fear itself."

34
00:04:25.065 --> 00:04:37.212
In the first hundred days of his presidency, FDR worked with Congress to bring about an economic recovery, but there was strong resistance from opponents who thought the government should not create jobs for the unemployed.

35
00:04:37.213 --> 00:04:39.537
But FDR prevailed.

36
00:04:39.538 --> 00:04:50.606
He called his idea the "New Deal," and created programs that provided jobs and a safety net for people in the form of Social Security and a federal minimum wage.

37
00:04:50.607 --> 00:04:54.936
In 1936, FDR was elected to his second term.

38
00:04:54.937 --> 00:05:01.204
Americans had grown to trust FDR during the Great Depression, because he spoke often and frankly to them.

39
00:05:01.205 --> 00:05:10.533
He took advantage of the latest technology in mass communication, the radio, to broadcast what became known as "fireside chats" into millions of homes.

40
00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:20.750
His leadership was especially critical during this time because Nazi Germany was planning to invade its European neighbors, and Japan (an ally of Germany) would soon attack China.

41
00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:28.411
By the time FDR won his third term in 1940, Nazi Germany had invaded France and was intent on crushing Great Britain.

42
00:05:28.412 --> 00:05:35.188
FDR sent aid to England in the form of weapons and supplies, but there was political resistance to his efforts.

43
00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:38.672
His critics wanted the United States to stay out of the war.

44
00:05:38.673 --> 00:05:44.784
But when the Japanese suddenly attacked the American navy fleet at Pearl Harbor in Hawaii, war became inevitable.

45
00:05:44.785 --> 00:05:54.827
The next day, FDR began one of his most famous speeches to Congress, "Yesterday, December 7, 1941—a date which will live in infamy . . .

46
00:05:54.828 --> 00:06:02.771
No matter how long it may take us to overcome this premeditated invasion, the American people . . . will win through to absolute victory."

47
00:06:02.772 --> 00:06:11.933
He rallied the country to war, and the number of American men and women in military service swelled to over 12 million by mid-1943.

48
00:06:11.934 --> 00:06:24.524
In 1942, with Japan an enemy and hysteria from the attack sweeping the country, FDR signed an executive order that required the creation of internment camps for Japanese-American citizens.

49
00:06:24.525 --> 00:06:29.863
One hundred and twenty thousand innocent civilians were imprisoned for the duration of the war.

50
00:06:29.864 --> 00:06:39.681
Historians see this as an unfortunate stain on FDR's legacy, but many people were suspicious of other Americans who had ties to countries at war with the US.

51
00:06:39.682 --> 00:06:44.840
In 1988, the United States formally apologized to the families who were imprisoned.

52
00:06:44.841 --> 00:06:50.055
A visibly tired FDR was re-elected to his fourth term in 1944.

53
00:06:50.056 --> 00:06:57.925
Even as the war raged, FDR looked to the future and worked tirelessly to plan the reconstruction of the world after the war's end.

54
00:06:57.926 --> 00:07:05.106
He worked to establish an organization through which international conflicts could be resolved, which would become the United Nations.

55
00:07:05.107 --> 00:07:14.641
This organization called for a world based on Four Freedoms: Freedom of Speech, Freedom of Worship, Freedom from Want, and Freedom from Fear.

56
00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:20.544
But by March of 1945, the 63-year-old FDR was frail and exhausted.

57
00:07:20.545 --> 00:07:27.966
Years of fighting the effects of polio, coupled with the complexity of leading a nation in crisis, had taken a brutal toll on him.

58
00:07:27.967 --> 00:07:35.363
In need of rest, he traveled to Warm Springs and died there of a stroke on April 12, 1945.

59
00:07:35.364 --> 00:07:43.520
A train carried FDR's casket to Washington DC, past millions of Americans who lined the railroad tracks in respectful silence.

60
00:07:43.521 --> 00:07:53.271
It was the end of the longest presidency in US history and the beginning of a new, postwar America, which had become the most powerful country in the world.
